## Graphical Abstract
Selective selenization of mixed-linker Ni-MOFs gave rise to NiSe2@NC core-shell nanooctahedrons. By changing the N-coordinating ligands in Ni-MOF precursors, the nitrogen content within the carbon shell was controlled. A strong correlation between pyridinic-N and HER activity was defined, leading to a material with HER activity approaching Pt.
## Highlights
•A series of NiSe2@NC core-shell nano-octahedrons with tunable N-species were obtained via changing the N-coordinating ligands in MOF precursors. •A strong correlation between pyridinic-N content and HER activity was identified. •The pyridinic-N induce interfacial charge redistribution on NiSe2 and N-doped carbon interfaces enhanced HER performance. •The NiSe2@NC-PZ exhibits superior HER performance and good long-term stability.
KEYWORDS: Transition metal selenides; Mixed-linker MOFs; Core-shell; Hydrogen evolution reaction
## 1. INTRODUCTION
Electrochemical water splitting via the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) represents an environment-friendly and high-efficient strategy for the hydrogen economy [1-3]. Pt-group metals (PGMs) have been considered as state-of-the-art electrocatalysts toward HER, but the low abundance and high cost impedes their large-scale applications. Thus, developing highly active electrocatalysts based on earth-abundant materials is desirable but remains challenging [4]. A broad range of catalysts based on non-noble materials such as chalcogenides, nitrides, carbides, and phosphides of transition metals have been studied as potential substitutes for PGMs [5-7]. Among them, transition metal selenides (TMSs) stand out due to its earth abundance and electrical conductivity [8]. However, further applications are still limited by the relatively low stability and poor activities of TMSs in alkaline conditions. Thus optimizing the surface electronic structures of TMSs for HER is in high demand. It has been demonstrated that hybridization with N-doped carbon materials can activate TMSs by creating extra localized reactive sites at the carbon-TMSs interfaces, while stabilizing the TMSs surface by avoiding direct contact with electrolytes [9-11]. Generally, the nitrogen species in N-doped carbons include pyridinic-N, pyrrole-N and graphite-N. For N-doped carbons, pyridinic-N could influence the electronic structure of carbon materials by increasing the density of p states near the Fermi level and reducing the work function, thus leading to enhanced activities for oxygen electrocatalysis [12-14]. However, there has been no systematic experimental and theoretical evidence to reveal the role of N-carbons in tuning the electronic structures and synergistic effects of the TMSs $@$ NC interface for HER electrocatalysis. A reason for this is the difficulty associated with the synthesis of TMSs $@$ NC materials equipped with controlled interfacial structures and tunable N-species. To this end, we propose the use of metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) as a platform for the controlled fabrication of TMSs $@$ NC materials.
MOFs are porous inorganic-organic-hybrid materials composed of metal nodes and organic ligands and they have been explored as precursors for various functional materials [15-19]. The existence of metals and carbon/nitrogen-containing ligands makes the MOFs an ideal platform with which to construct composites with metal-containing nanoparticles embedded in N-doped porous carbon. In a typical synthesis of such nanohybrid materials, MOFs are usually pyrolyzed under an inert atmosphere. For example, CoP@NC was synthesized by pyrolysis of a ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ - benzimidazole containing MOF (ZIF-9), followed by phosphidation treatment [20]. Similarly, ${\mathrm{Ni}}{\mathrm{Se}}_{2}@{\mathrm{NC}}$ can be obtained from a Ni-based MOF via pyrolysis and selenization [21]. The porous nature of MOFs allows the formation of a porous architecture of metal compounds supported on carbon, facilitating the electrocatalytic applications. However, the irregular morphology of metal compounds hinders the identification of active sites. Moreover, it is usually difficult to control the nitrogen species and their contents in the NC-supports in a direct pyrolysis process.
Herein, we selected a series of mixed-linker Ni-MOFs as precursors for the fabrication of uniform TMSs $@$ NC core-shell nano-octahedrons with well-defined interfacial structures and controlled N-doped carbon compositions. These mixed-linker Ni-MOFs are composed of Ocoordinating anionic carboxylate linkers and N-coordinating neutral ligands. Initially, the Ocoordinating anionic carboxylate linkers could be easily replaced by ${\mathrm{Se}_{2}}^{2-}$ anions during selective selenization process, while the neutral N-coordinating ligands are maintained and embedded in the NiSe2 nanocrystals. During the carbonization process at $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\$ , the N-ligands are converted into ultrathin N-doped carbon and segregated onto the $\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{Se}_{2}$ surface. The N concentration and pyridinic-N contents were controlled by varying the N-ligands in MOFs (Nligands $\mathit{\Theta}=4{,}4^{\prime}$ -bipyridine, 1,4-diazabicyclooctane, pyrazine, aminopyrazine), thus providing a highly controllable platform for mechanistic studies. It was found that the HER activities in alkaline media increases with the contents of pyridinic-N. The optimized core-shell ${\mathrm{NiSe}}_{2}@{\mathrm{NC}}$ nano-octahedron with pyridinic-N content amounts to $63.4\%$ of the total nitrogen content, termed as NiSe2@NC-PZ, and shows the highest HER activity with a low overpotential of $162\mathrm{mV}$ at 10 mA·cm-2 in alkaline media. Density functional theory (DFT) analysis suggests that the interaction between NiSe2 and N-doped carbon induces charge redistribution on their coupling interfaces. And pyridinic-N plays a pivotal role in regulating the interfacial electronic structure of NiSe2@NC-PZ nanohybrids and promoting the adsorption of reaction intermediates, thereby improving the HER performance.
## 2. EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
## 2.1 Materials and reagents
All the chemical reagents were of analytical grade purity and used without further purification. Nickel (II) nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ , trimesic acid $\mathrm{\langleH_{3}B T C\rangle}$ ), 4,4'- bipyridine (BP), 1,4-diazabicyclooctane (DO), pyrazine (PZ), aminopyrazine (AE), N,Ndimethylformamide (DMF), selenium powder (Se), hydrazine hydrate $(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ , commercial platinum/carbon (Pt/C) $20\mathrm{wt}\%$ Pt on Vulcan XC-72R), Nafion solution $(5\mathrm{~wt\%})$ .
## 2.2  Synthesis of Ni-MOFs
Ni-MOF-PZ was synthesized under the facile solvothermal conditions previously reported, with some modifications. First, 0.5 mmol of Ni(NO3)2· $6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , 0.5 mmol H3BTC and 0.5 mmol PZ were dissolved in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF solution. The mixture was further stirred until completely dissolved at room temperature for 30 minutes. Then the green solution was transferred to a 25 mL Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave, and kept at $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $4320\mathrm{min}$ . Finally, the resulting precipitate was separated by centrifugation with a large amount of DMF and methanol to remove unreacted residue for several times. The final Ni-MOF-PZ was obtained by drying in a vacuum at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . The preparation of Ni-MOF-BP, Ni-MOF-DO and Ni-MOF-AE were similar to that of Ni-MOF-PZ, except that BP, DO, and AE were corresponding used.
2.3 Synthesis of NiSe2 and NiSe2@NC nanohybrids.
NiSe2@NC-PZ was synthesized via a hydrothermal method and followed calcination process. Typically, $50~\mathrm{mg}$ Ni-MOF-PZ were dissolved in deionized water ( $\mathrm{10~mL}$ ) and selenium powder $\cdot1.5\ \mathrm{mmol};$ ) was added into hydrazine hydrate $\mathrm{5.0~mL}$ , $85\%$ ) in open air. Then, the hydrazine hydrate-Se solution was added into the MOF-water solution drop by drop by vigorously stirring at room temperature. After $180~\mathrm{{min}}$ , the mixture was heated at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 12 h in a $23\mathrm{\mL}$ Teflon-lined autoclave. The PZ@NiSe2 was obtained after cooling to room temperature, centrifuging the suspension and freeze-drying. And the final $\mathrm{NiSe}_{2}@\mathrm{NC-PZ}$ was obtained by annealing $\mathrm{P}Z@\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{Se}_{2}$ at $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ under ${\bf N}_{2}$ flow with the heating rate of $1\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}$ . The preparation of bare NiSe2, NiSe2@NC-BP, NiSe2@NC-DO, and NiSe2@NC-AE were similar to that of ${\mathrm{NiSe}}_{2}@{\mathrm{NC}}-{\mathrm{P}}_{4}^{\prime}$ Z except for the use of $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , Ni-MOF-BP, NiMOF-DO and Ni-MOF-AE as the metal source.
## 2.4  Materials Characterization
X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were obtained using a Bruker AXS D8 with Cu Kα radiation $(\lambda=1.5418\mathrm{~\AA~})$ . The thermos gravimetric (TG) measurements were used by Mettler Toledo thermal analyzer with a heating rate of $10\ \mathrm{^{\circ}C\ m i n^{-1}}$ in the range of $40{-}800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ atmosphere. The surface areas and pore size distribution of the synthesized samples were determined by the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) method and the Barrett Joyner-Halenda (BJH) model using a Tristar 2460 surface area analyzer. Field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM) images were obtained on a Hitachi SU-8000 instrument. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) was performed using a Hitachi JEM-2100F instrument. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra were acquired by scanning X-ray microprobe (Thermo Scientific, ESCALAB 250Xi) using Al Kα radiation. All binding energies were calibrated via using the C 1s peak at 284.8 eV as the internal standard. Raman spectra were recorded on a HORIBA Evolution Raman scope with the excitation wavelength of $532\mathrm{nm}$ . $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectra were carried out on an Avance3hd 600 MHz spectrometer (Bruker, Switzerland).
## 2.5  Electrochemical measurements
Electrocatalytic measurements were controlled by electrochemical workstation (Gamry Reference 09209) in a standard three-electrode system using graphite rod as the counter electrode, an KCl-saturated Ag/AgCl electrode as the reference electrode and the sample modified glassy carbon electrode (GCE) as the working electrode. All the polarization curves were recorded with iR compensation. Typically, $5.0~\mathrm{mg}$ of as-prepared samples were dispersed into $0.5~\mathrm{mL}$ mixture solution of Nafion $5\%$ (w/w)), DI water and ethanol solution (volume ratio is 1: 9: 10) to form a homogeneous ink with the assistance of sonication. Then ${5}~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ inks were drop-casted on a polished GCE with a diameter of $3\mathrm{mm}$ . Then the electrode was allowed to dry at room temperature for two hours before measurements (loading: $0.35\mathrm{\mg{\cdot}c m^{-2}}$ ). Polarization curves for HER activities were characterized by linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) with a scan rate of $5\mathrm{mVs^{-1}}$ . All potentials measured were calibrated to RHE by adding of $(0.22+0.059\mathrm{pH})$ + unless stated otherwise. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was obtained by applying an AC voltage with $10~\mathrm{mV}$ amplitude and recorded at -0.16 V vs. RHE. The cyclic voltammogram measurements (CV) at various scan rates $\mathrm{20{-}100\ m V\ s^{-1}}$ ) were carried out at non-faradaic overpotentials. The ECSA was calculated by the double layer capacitance $\left(\mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{dl}}\right)$ . Afterwards, the electrochemical stability was assessed by repeated cyclic voltammetry (CV) and potential-time curves at $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ with a scanning rate of $200\mathrm{mVs^{-1}}$ .
