## 3. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
## 3.1 MOF based synthesis of NiSe2@NC core-shell nano-octahedrons
The ${\mathrm{NiSe}}_{2}@{\mathrm{NC}}$ nanohybrids were assembled by a MOF-based strategy via solvothermal selenization followed by calcination, shown in Fig. 1. First, mixed-linker Ni-MOFs were synthesized utilizing ${\mathrm{Ni}}({\mathrm{NO}}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{\cdot}$ , O-donor ligands $\mathrm{{\smallH_{3}L1}~=~}$ trimesic acid) and N-donor ancillary ligands (L2) following slightly modified published procedures [27-29]. A series of isostructural Ni-MOFs were obtained using different L2 with variable N contents, including 4,4'- bipyridine (BP), 1,4-diazabicyclooctane (DO), pyazine (PZ), and aminopyrazine (AE). The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) and Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) results together with corresponding optical photographs confirmed the successful synthesis of mixed-linker Ni-MOFs (Figs. S1-3). The resultant Ni-MOFs were subsequently selenized by reacting with Se powder under solvothermal conditions at $100^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ resulting in N-rich L2 embedded NiSe2 nanooctahedrons, denoted as $\mathrm{L}2@\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{Se}_{2}$ , where $\mathrm{L}2=\mathrm{BP}$ , DO, PZ, or AE. During further calcination process, the N-rich ligands (L2) began to carbonize, forming uniform N-doped carbon-coated NiSe2 nano-octahedrons, denoted as NiSe2@NC-BP, NiSe2@NC-DO, NiSe2@NC-PZ and NiSe2@NC-AE, respectively.
The morphologies of $\mathrm{P}Z@\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{Se}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NiSe}_{2}@$ NC-PZ were compared by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images. Both PZ@NiSe2 and NiSe2@NC-PZ exhibited a typical similarly sized octahedral shape with edge length of ca. 150 nm (Fig. 2a and 2b). A thin carbon layer $(\sim1.5\ \mathrm{nm})$ ) was clearly observed on the surface of NiSe2@NC-PZ (Fig. 2c and 2d), but not observed in PZ $@$ NiSe2. This is attributed to the transformation of N-rich PZ ligands into an N-doped carbon layer covering the $\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{Se}_{2}$ surface. The high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) image of the both materials showed a $0.243~\mathrm{nm}$ spaced lattice fringe indexed to the (211) face of the cubic NiSe2 phase (Fig. 2e and 2f) [30]. The selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern indicates the single crystalline nature of $\mathrm{P}Z@\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{Se}_{2}$ (Fig. 2g), which became polycrystalline after calcination, forming NiSe2@NC-PZ (Fig. 2h). A series of well-defined rings in the NiSe2@NC-PZ sample can be assigned to the (111), (211), (031) and (320) planes of the cubic NiSe2 phase [31]. Elemental mapping images reveal the uniform distribution of the Se, Ni, C and N elements throughout the $\mathrm{P}Z@\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{Se}_{2}$ and NiSe2@NC-PZ nanohybrids (Fig. 2i and S4).
The PXRD patterns of the bare NiSe2, PZ $@$ NiSe2 and NiSe2@NC-PZ nanohybrids (Fig. S5) are fully in line with the standard $\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{Se}_{2}$ (cubic, Pa3, PDF#88-1711) [32]. No impurity peaks are observed, suggesting that the introduction of N-doped carbon did not change the cubic phase of NiSe2. As shown in the Raman spectra (Fig. 3a), four peaks centered at 149, 166, 207 and 238 $\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ attribute to the stretching and relaxation modes of Se−Se pairs. The peaks at 1380 and 1580 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ correspond to the D-band and G-band of the carbon materials. These bands are found only in PZ@NiSe2 and NiSe2@NC-PZ nanohybrids due to the selective selenization process. Moreover, the intensity ratio of the D and G bands for $\mathrm{NiSe}_{2}@\mathrm{NC-PZ}$ nanohybrids is around 0.98, compared with 0.87 in the case of $\mathrm{PZ}@\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{Se}_{2}$ , indicating the appearance of the structural defects during the transformation of N-rich ligands to N-doped carbon materials [33,34]. In addition, the $\mathrm{NiSe}_{2}@\mathrm{NC-PZ}$ nanohybrids exhibit a larger BET surface area of $49.2~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ compared with those of bare $\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{Se}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{PZ}@\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{Se}_{2}$ , which are 6.3 and $12.5~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ respectively, according to ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms (Fig. S6). The derived pore size distribution of the $\mathrm{NiSe}_{2}@\mathrm{NC-PZ}$ nanohybrids indicates relatively uniform ${\sim}1.8~\mathrm{nm}$ micropores, which supports rapid mass/charge transportation and provides abundant surface-active sites when these nanohybrids are used as electrode materials [35].
To investigate the transformation process of Ni-MOF-PZ to NiSe2@NC-PZ nanooctahedrons, the material compositions at each step of the synthesis (i.e. Ni-MOF-PZ, $\mathrm{P}Z@\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{Se}_{2}$ and ${\mathrm{Ni}}{\mathrm{Se}}_{2}@ $ NC-PZ) were analyzed by $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR, thermogravimetric analysis (TGA), and elemental analysis. The $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectra of acid digested Ni-MOF-PZ indicated the coexistence of L1 and PZ in a 1:1 molar ratio, matching well with the crystal structure of Ni-MOF (Fig. S7a). After the selenization process, the L1 was not observed by $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR while the PZ content was unchanged, signifying that $\mathrm{Se}_{2}{}^{2-}$ had selectively replaced the anionic L1 while maintaining neutral PZ ligands (Fig. S7b). The carbonized product, $\mathrm{NiSe}_{2}@\mathrm{NC-PZ}$ , did not show any $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR signals (Fig. S7c), suggesting a complete transformation of the molecular PZ ligand into N-doped carbon. TGA curves of all samples were collected under an oxygen atmosphere within a temperature range of 40 to $700~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at a heating rate of $10\ \mathrm{^{\circ}C\cdot m i n^{-1}}$ . NiMOF-PZ showed a weight loss of $72.8\%$ before $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , corresponding to the decomposition of L1 and PZ. PZ@NiSe2 exhibited weight losses at $\mathord{\sim}400$ and ${\sim}600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ , which were attributed to the decomposition of PZ and $\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{Se}_{2}$ , respectively. The $\mathrm{NiSe}_{2}@\mathrm{NC-PZ}$ nanohybrid also showed a two-step decomposition process at 550 and $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , in line with the decomposition of NiSe2 and N-doped carbons. By comparison with the TGA curves of the bare $\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{Se}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{PZ}@\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{Se}_{2}$ and
The use of mixed-linker Ni-MOFs as precursors is important for the formation of $\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{Se}_{2}@\mathrm{NC}$ nano-octahedrons. For comparison, control experiments were conducted by directly mixing $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , H3L1 and L2 ( $\mathrm{PZ}$ as an example) under the solvothermal selenization conditions, which do not form uniform L2 embedded NiSe2 nano-octahedrons. Furthermore, the L1 ligand ensures the formation of porous mixed-linker Ni-MOFs, and this facilitates the diffusion of Se species during selenization process. Control experiments were conducted by replacing the mixed-linker Ni-MOF with a coordination polymer formed merely by $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ and L2, but this coordination polymer fails to react completely with the Se species due to the limited porosity. To further investigate the template effect of Ni-MOFs during selenization, the timedependent morphological evolution of the Ni-MOF into $\mathrm{P}Z@\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{Se}_{2}$ nano-octahedrons was monitored by SEM. The gradual transformation of large MOF particles into a regular nanooctahedron is observed within 1 h (Fig. S9). These results indicate that the porous mixed linker Ni-MOFs act as templates to facilitate the incorporation of N-ligands in $\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{Se}_{2}$ nano-octahedrons.
Taking advantage of the high tunability of MOFs, a series of isostructural MOFs with variable N-contents were used as precursors, giving rise to ${\mathrm{Ni}}{\mathrm{Se}}_{2}@{\mathrm{NC}}$ core-shell nanooctahedrons with similar size but different N-species. X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS) were employed to analyze the electronic structural characteristics of the ${\mathrm{Ni}}{\mathrm{Se}}_{2}@{\mathrm{NC}}$ nanohybrids (Fig. S10a). Taking NiSe2@NC-PZ as an example, in the Se 3d spectrum (Fig. S10b), two peaks located at 53.1 and $54.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ correspond to Se $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ and Se $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ [38]. In the C 1s region of the spectrum, two peaks were observed at 284.6 and $285.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ , which are attributed to $C^{\mathrm{-}}C$ and $\mathrm{C-N/C=N}$ bonds, respectively (Fig. S10c) [39]. The N 1s spectrum is shown in Fig. S10d. The characteristic peaks around 398.9, 400.2 and $402.2\mathrm{eV}$ can be assigned to pyridinic- N, pyrrolicN and the graphitic-N. NiSe2@NC-PZ shows a binding energy of N 1s higher than that of other ${\mathrm{Ni}}{\mathrm{Se}}_{2}@{\mathrm{NC}}$ nanohybrids, which might suggest stronger electronic interactions between NiSe2 and N-doped carbon. The strong interaction can be further evidenced by the Ni 2p spectrum (Fig. 3c). The peaks located at $855.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $873.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ can be assigned to $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}2\mathrm{p}3/2$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}2\mathrm{p}1/2$ . The corresponding satellite peaks appear at $879.3{\mathrm{~eV}}$ and 861.1 eV [40,41]. The slightly weaker peaks at $852.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $870.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ can be attributed to $\mathrm{Ni^{0}}$ originating from the reduction of hydrazine hydrate. For the ${\mathrm{NiSe}}_{2}@{\mathrm{NC}}$ series, the binding energies of nickel ions negative shifts to lower binding energy. Among them, NiSe2@NC-PZ nanohybrids exhibits the largest negative shift of about $0.84\ \mathrm{~eV}$ , compared with that of 0.17, 0.38, 0.51 eV, respectively. These observations suggest that interfacial charge transfer may exist from Ni $(\delta+)$ to N (δ-) in ${\mathrm{Ni}}{\mathrm{Se}}_{2}@{\mathrm{NC}}$ nanohybrids, especially for the $\mathrm{NiSe}_{2}@\mathrm{NC-PZ}$ nanohybrids [42,43]. The contents of the N species were also compared by XPS. It was observed that although the total $\mathbf{N}$ contents in $\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{Se}_{2}@\mathrm{NC}$ $5.72\%$ , $10.88\%$ , $13.55\%$ , and $15.54\%$ for ${\mathrm{NiSe}}_{2}@{\mathrm{NC}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{BP}}$ , -DO, -PZ, and -AE, respectively) is positively correlated to the initial nitrogen content of the L2 ( $17.93\%$ , $12.48\%$ , $35.0\%$ , and $44.16\%$ for BP, DO, PZ, and AE respectively), and the relative ratios of pyridinic-N reached a maximum of $63.4\ \mathrm{wt}\%$ in $\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{Se}_{2}@$ NC-PZ (Fig. 3d). Interestingly, the pyridinic-N contents are correlated to the value of Ni-shift, as observed in the Fig. 3d. These results suggest that the improved pyridinic-N ratio in the NiSe2@NC-PZ nanohybrids will enhance charge transfer between N-doped carbon and $\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{Se}_{2}$ , leading to the redistribution of charge on their coupling interfaces, indicating promising electrochemical catalytic performance [44].
The electrocatalytic HER performances were investigated in ${\bf N}_{2}$ -saturated 1.0 M KOH solution using a typical three-electrode configuration. Fig. 4a displays the linear sweep voltammograms (LSV) curves after $i R$ correction of bare $\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{Se}_{2}$ and ${\mathrm{NiSe}}_{2}@{\mathrm{NC}}$ with different pyridinic-N contents. All of them shows improved HER activity compared to bare NiSe2. Among all the ${\mathrm{Ni}}{\mathrm{Se}}_{2}@{\mathrm{NC}}$ nanohybrids, the $\mathrm{NiSe}_{2}@\mathrm{NC-PZ}$ show the highest activity with an overpotential of $162~\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ in comparison with that of the NiSe2@NC-BP (235 mV), NiSe2@NC-DO $208~\mathrm{mV}$ ), ${\mathrm{Ni}}{\mathrm{Se}}_{2}@ $ NC-AE ( $182\mathrm{mV},$ ) and bare $\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{Se}_{2}$ ( $283\ \mathrm{mV},$ ). The SEM images shows that all the $\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{Se}_{2}@\mathrm{NC}$ nano-octahedrons have similar particle sizes and uniform size distributions, eliminating the effect of particle sizes on the HER activity (Fig. S11). The fitted low Tafel slope $(88\mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}},$ ) of the $\mathrm{NiSe}_{2}@\mathrm{NC-PZ}$ indicates faster reaction kinetics than those for other ${\mathrm{NiSe}}_{2}@{\mathrm{NC}}$ nanohybrids (Fig. 4b) [45]. Meanwhile, it suggests that the HER occurs through a Volmer-Heyrovsky combination mechanism (a Tafel slope between 40 and 120 $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{V}{\cdot}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}.$ ) [46]. The overpotentials at current densities of $10\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ was plotted against the ratio of different N-species (obtained from XPS data). The HER activities in alkaline media showed a linear correlation with the contents of pyridinic-N of ${\mathrm{Ni}}{\mathrm{Se}}_{2}@{\mathrm{NC}}$ nanohybrids (Fig. 4c), whereas the effects of pyrrolic-N and graphitic-N were less obvious (Fig. S12), indicating that the HER activity of core-shell $\mathrm{NiSe}_{2}@\mathrm{NC}$ nanohybrids in alkaline conditions are predominantly determined by the pyridinic-N contents. Cyclic voltammograms (CV) curves with multiple scan rates were employed to estimate the electrochemical double-layer capacitance $\mathrm{(C_{dl})}$ (Fig. S13). The $\mathrm{\bfC}_{\mathrm{dl}}$ for bare $\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{Se}_{2}$ and ${\mathrm{Ni}}{\mathrm{Se}}_{2}@{\mathrm{NC}}$ nanohybrids are 3.12, 3.98, 4.18, 5.88, and $4.94\mathrm{\mF\cm^{-2}}$ , respectively, indicating that NiSe2@NC-PZ nanohybrids possess slightly more available surface sites (Fig. 4d). The corresponding electrochemical active surface areas (ECSA) are estimated to be 3.67, 4.68, 4.92, 6.92, and $5.81\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , respectively, according to the equation $\mathrm{ECSA}=\mathrm{C_{dl}}/\mathrm{C_{s}}$ where $\mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{s}}$ represent the specific capacitance [47]. ECSA is only a measure of the number of electrochemical active sites, and not all active sites are catalytically active. To compare the intrinsic activity of active sites, the polarization curves were normalized by ECSA. As shown in Fig. 4e and Fig. S14, NiSe2@NC-PZ displayed the lowest overpotential and Tafel slope, indicating a higher intrinsic activity. The HER performance of NiSe2@NC-PZ had surpassed most of the noble-metal-free HER electrocatalysts in alkaline media (Table S7).
Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) revealed lower resistance for ${\mathrm{NiSe}}_{2}@{\mathrm{NC}}-$ PZ nanohybrids, which is favorable for charge transport kinetics (Fig. S15). The HER curve of NiSe2@NC-PZ nanohybrids remained unaltered after 2000 cycles (Fig. 4f) and no obvious morphology changes were observed (Fig. S16 and 17). Time dependent current density (i-t)
curve obtained at $10\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ shows negligible current density decay after a $40\mathrm{~h~}$ electrolysis test. Both of these observations reveal the excellent stability of $\mathrm{NiSe}_{2}@\mathrm{NC-PZ}$ nanohybrids in alkaline media. In addition, the HER performance of ${\mathrm{NiSe}}_{2}@$ NC-PZ and bare $\mathrm{Ni}\mathsf{S}\mathsf{e}_{2}$ was further investigated in acidic and neutral media. As illustrated in Figs. S18-20, NiSe2@NC-PZ and bare NiSe2 achieves a current density of $10\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at overpotentials of around $183\mathrm{mV}$ and $245\mathrm{mV}$ , respectively. As expected, $\mathrm{NiSe}_{2}@\mathrm{NC-PZ}$ also displays a low Tafel slope of 37 mV dec−1 compared with ${53\ \mathrm{mV\cdotdec}^{-1}}$ for bare $\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{Se}_{2}$ . This indicates that the N-doped carbon shell and NiSe2 synergistically increase the electron density on the carbon surface and, in turn, improve the HER activity at all $\mathrm{pH}$ values. Furthermore, a much smaller contact angle was observed for the NiSe2@NC-PZ $\left(\theta=28.5^{\circ}\right),$ ), indicating its hydrophilic nature (Fig. S21) [48]. The increase in surface wettability facilitates the access of the electrolyte to catalytic-active sites, improving the rate of hydrogen evolution.
## 3.4 Mechanistic understanding by DFT
## 4. CONCLUSION
In conclusion, ${\mathrm{Ni}}{\mathrm{Se}}_{2}@{\mathrm{NC}}$ core-shell nano-octahedrons with tunable N-contents have been synthesized from a series of MOF precursors. By tuning the composition of N-doped carbon shells, the electronic structures of the $\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{Se}_{2}$ surface were modified and a strong correlation between pyridinic-N and HER activity was identified. The NiSe2@NC-PZ nanohybrids with maximum pyridinic-N content $(63.4\%)$ exhibited the highest HER performance with a low overpotential of $162~\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and good stability (up to $40\mathrm{{h}}$ ) in 1.0 M KOH solution. DFT calculations indicate that pyridinic-N induces charge redistribution between the $\mathrm{Ni}\mathsf{S e}_{2}$ surface and N-doped carbon, and further promote the interfacial electronic structure modulation of ${\mathrm{Ni}}{\mathrm{Se}}_{2}@{\mathrm{NC}}$ nanohybrids, resulting in an optimized adsorption energy of hydrogen $(0.08{\mathrm{~eV}})$ . This work not only identifies the critical role of pyridinic-N in regulating surface electronic structure of $\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{Se}_{2}$ for improved HER performance, but also provides a highly tunable and welldefined platform for the design of future catalysts. Further works are in progress to expand the composition of exploratory TMSs $@$ NC nanohybrids and explore their application in a wide scope of reactions including OER, ORR, and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction.
